<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="981571dc" lines="6-19">
<description>
Reconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.

Input structure:
- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.
- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.

Rule:
- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.
- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.
- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.

Key insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="db7260a4" lines="37-56">
<description>
Drop the singleton marker into the matching red U-shaped container, or make a bottom-row puddle if no closed container is hit.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background, one singleton marker pixel on the top row,
  and one other foreground color forming vertical bars and bottom segments.
- A closed container is a U shape: two vertical side bars with a continuous
  bottom segment between them and an empty rectangular interior above the bottom.

Rule
- Remove the top marker from its original cell.
- If exactly one closed U-shaped container has the marker column within its
  horizontal footprint, fill that container's empty interior with the marker color.
- If no closed container is under the marker column, fill the whole bottom row
  with the marker color.

Key insight
- The marker does not identify a color by value; it identifies a vertical drop
  column, and only a structurally closed U shape can catch and contain the fill.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="e84fef15" lines="39-59">
<description>
Reconstruct the repeated tile from its separated copies and mark the missing pixels in the damaged copy blue.

Input structure:
- The input is a lattice of equal-size blocks separated by one-cell solid rows
  and columns of the same separator color.
- All blocks are copies of one motif except exactly one block, where some
  motif pixels have been overwritten by the motif background color.
- Blue is absent from the input and is reserved as the output marker color.

Rule:
- Recover the motif by taking the unique majority color at each local block
  coordinate across all block copies.
- Find the single block that differs from this consensus motif.
- Return the consensus motif, recoloring the differing local coordinates blue.

Key insight:
- The repeated copies provide redundancy: the damaged positions are exactly
  the coordinates where the lone nonconforming copy has background instead of
  the consensus motif color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="538b439f" lines="122-142">
<description>
Reflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel
  separator line in a non-background color.
- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.
- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.

Rule:
- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.
- Mirror each source object across the separator line.
- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the
  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the
  reflected object in the source color.

Key insight:
- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified
  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while
  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="aa62e3f4" lines="18-35">
<description>
Replace the outlined object with the outside contour of that outline in the marker color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background surrounds one 8-connected foreground outline.
- The outline is the unique largest foreground color.
- One or more smaller interior colors are inside the outline; the marker color
  is the unique smallest non-outline color.

Rule:
- Erase all original foreground pixels to background.
- Paint every background pixel that is 4-adjacent to the outline with the
  marker color.
- Keep the grid shape unchanged.

Key insight: The colored interior marker supplies the paint color, while the
largest outline supplies only the contour whose outside edge is traced.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="1e5d6875" lines="26-45">
<description>
Paint a recolored diagonal copy from each L triomino source object.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The only foreground objects are 4-connected red and grey L triominoes,
  each occupying three pixels of a 2x2 box.

Rule:
- Grey L objects paint a yellow copy translated one diagonal step toward
  their missing 2x2 corner.
- Red L objects paint a green copy translated one diagonal step away from
  their missing 2x2 corner.
- Copies are clipped by the grid boundary; red-derived green copies are
  painted after grey-derived yellow copies where the projections collide.

Key insight:
- The missing corner of each 2x2 L object identifies its diagonal axis, and
  the source color determines which side of that axis receives the copy.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="6e453dd6" lines="39-52">
<description>
Slide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.

Input structure:
- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.
- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).

Rule:
- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.
- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.
- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.

Key insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as "nozzles" projecting rightward.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="8e301a54" lines="8-23">
<description>
Move every foreground object straight downward by a number of rows equal to its own pixel count.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- The foreground is made of separated 4-connected monochromatic objects.
- Objects may move partly below the bottom edge of the grid.

Rule:
- Clear the canvas to the background.
- For each foreground object, compute its area in pixels.
- Redraw the object at the same columns and that many rows lower, clipping pixels below the grid.

Key insight:
- The downward displacement is the object's own area; color and shape only identify which pixels move together.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="b9e38dc0" lines="7-18">
<description>
Fill the region enclosed by a wall sprite and radiate color outward through its opening.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Wall = most frequent non-bg color, forming a partial enclosure (e.g. U-shape).
- A fill-color pixel sits inside the enclosed area. Other non-bg pixels may act as obstacles.
Rule:
- Flood-fill the enclosed area with the fill color, radiating outward through the wall's open end.
- Obstacles cast "shadows" along the radiate direction, blocking fill behind them.

Key insight: The wall endpoints define the opening direction; fill radiates outward through it, and obstacles block propagation along that same axis.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="6d1d5c90" lines="8-23">
<description>
Remove the left control column and roll the remaining image downward by the red marker's row.

Input structure
- The input is a rectangular grid with a one-cell-wide control column on the left.
- That control column is all pink except for exactly one red marker pixel.
- The columns to the right are the image that remains in the output.

Rule
- Delete the left control column.
- Let r be the row index of the red marker in that column.
- Cyclically shift the remaining image downward by r rows.

Key insight
- The red marker's vertical position encodes the row-roll amount; the pink
  column is only a control strip and is removed after reading it.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

